# Git workflow

## Branching

- **One long-lived branch: `main`.** It is always deployable and protected. A repo has exactly one default branch — not `main` and `master` side by side.
- Work happens on short-lived branches cut from `main`, and merged back within a day or two. A branch that lives a week is a merge conflict being saved up.
- Name branches `type/short-description`, with the same types as commits: `feat/budget-rollover`, `fix/login-redirect-loop`, `chore/upgrade-mantine`.
- Unfinished work behind a flag on `main` beats a long-running branch.

## Commits

Conventional Commits, always:

```
type(scope): imperative subject, lower case, no full stop, 72 characters or fewer

Why the change was needed and what trade-off it makes. The diff already
shows what changed.

Closes #123
```

| Type | For |
| --- | --- |
| `feat` | A new capability for the user |
| `fix` | A bug fix |
| `refactor` | A structural change with no behaviour change |
| `perf` | A measured performance improvement |
| `test` | Tests only |
| `docs` | Documentation only |
| `build` / `ci` | Build system, dependencies, pipelines |
| `chore` | Maintenance that fits nothing above |
| `revert` | Reverting an earlier commit |

- **Scope** is the feature or package: `auth`, `budgets`, `web`, `api`, `db`. Use the same small set every time.
- A breaking change is marked with `!` (`feat(api)!: …`) and a `BREAKING CHANGE:` footer.
- **One logical change per commit.** It builds and passes on its own. Refactoring and behaviour changes go in separate commits, and so do formatting-only changes.
- Write the subject so it completes "This commit will …". `fix stuff`, `wip` and `updates` are not messages.
- Review your own staged diff before committing (`git diff --staged`): no debug output, no commented-out code, no unrelated edits.

## History

- Update your branch by rebasing on `main`: `git pull --rebase origin main`.
- Rewrite only your own unshared branches, and push with `--force-with-lease`, never `--force`.
- Never rewrite `main`, or any branch someone else has pulled.
- Pull requests are **squash-merged**. The PR title becomes the commit on `main`, so it follows the commit format. Delete the branch after merging.

## Pull requests

- Small and single-purpose — a reviewer should finish in one sitting (roughly 400 changed lines or fewer). Split by slice, not by layer.
- Open a draft early. Mark it ready only when CI is green and you have reviewed your own diff.
- The description answers:
  ```markdown
  ## What and why
  ## How it was tested
  ## Screenshots (for UI changes)
  ## Risk and rollback
  Closes #123
  ```
- Respond to every comment. The author merges once approved.
- Reviewers check behaviour, boundaries and tests before style. Lint already handles style.

## Never committed

- `.env` and any real secret, key or certificate. Only `.env.example` with placeholders is committed.
- `node_modules`, build output (`dist`, `.next`), coverage, generated clients, logs.
- Editor and OS files, local databases, personal data files, large binaries.

Keep `.gitignore` short and specific to this stack, and add `.gitattributes` with `* text=auto eol=lf`.

**If a secret is committed:** rotate it first, then remove it from history. Deleting the line in a later commit does nothing — the secret is still in the repository, and should be treated as leaked.

## Releases

- Versions are `vMAJOR.MINOR.PATCH` tags on `main`.
- The changelog is generated from commit messages (release-please or changesets), which is the reason the commit format matters.

## Rules for an agent working in the repo

- Do not commit or push unless asked.
- Never commit directly to the default branch. Create a branch first.
- Stage by path. Do not `git add -A` over files you have not looked at.
- Never bypass hooks with `--no-verify`. Fix what the hook reports.
- Never force-push a shared branch, and never rewrite history that has been pushed, without explicit approval.
- Leave untracked files you did not create alone.

## Smells to fix on sight

- Two default branches in one repository.
- Commits titled identically, or with no type.
- A pull request that mixes a refactor, a feature and a dependency upgrade.
- A branch more than a few days behind `main`.
- A secret, a spreadsheet of personal data, or a build folder in `git status`.
